{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_1","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/bash -lc 'rg -n -i \"palette|categorical\" docs/01-landscape.md'","aggregated_output":"10:2. **Accessibility depends on work the author rarely does.** Tested as the other nine were, Highcharts exposes one named image and no keyboard access; with its accessibility module imported it is the most complete of the ten. Three libraries (Recharts, ApexCharts, MUI X) had keyboard navigation on by default in the versions tested. Five exposed nothing a keyboard user could reach. Four keep animating bars under reduced motion. No default palette both reached 3:1 contrast and kept its colors apart under simulated color-vision deficiency.\n123:| `parts` | 6 shares, two under 3% | Part-to-whole labels, palette |\n220:**R7. Default palettes fail contrast, color-vision checks, or both.** The colors of the six donut slices, in order, scored for contrast against white (WCAG 1.4.11 asks 3:1 for graphical objects that are needed to understand the content) and for the smallest pairwise difference in OKLab under normal vision and Machado-simulated deficiencies. Repeated colors count as a pair with zero difference.\n225:| Recharts (documentation colors; it has no palette) | `#0088fe` `#00c49f` `#ffbb28` `#ff8042` `#0088fe` `#00c49f` | 4 | 4 | 0 | 0 | 0 | 0 |\n233:ApexCharts is the only one whose colors all reach 3:1, and its sixth slice has the same blue as its first, next to it on the ring. Recharts has no palette (every line is `#3182bd` and every sector `#808080` until the author assigns colors); the repeat above comes from cycling the four colors its examples use, which is **authored**. uPlot has no palette. Plotly's orange and green are nearly identical under simulated protanopia (0.007). ECharts was scored from its option palette, not from rendered pixels.\n235:A search under the same metric found eight-color sets that reach 3:1 and keep a minimum difference of 0.12 on white and 0.14 on a dark background ([`research/palette/feasibility.json`](../research/palette/feasibility.json)). Those sets cover few hues, differ between light and dark, and were scored as swatches, not as 2 px lines. So the thresholds are reachable; whether a palette people would choose meets them is open.\n243:**R10. Following the page's color scheme is rare, and following the device is not the same thing.** On a page that follows `prefers-color-scheme: dark`, two charts stayed legible without configuration: Highcharts 13, which switches palette and text, and the authored D3 chart, whose axis text uses the inherited color. On a page that declares itself light-only, viewed on a device set to dark, Highcharts drew a black chart on the white page: it follows the device preference, not the page. The other nine stayed light there. **Inferred**: a chart should take its scheme from the page it is in.\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
