{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_38","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/bash -lc \"rg --files --hidden --no-ignore research/harness | rg -i \\\"Tableau10|observable10|Carbon|Okabe\\\" | head -45 && rg -n -i -w 'd3' docs/01-landscape.md | rg -i \\\"color|palette|author\\\" | head -16\"","aggregated_output":"22:| **Authored** | The result of code I wrote for the task (all of D3; parts of uPlot's bars and Recharts' colors), not of a library default. |\n89:So six of the ten are supported by every signal, two by some, and two are judgment calls. D3 has no chart-level API; its tasks are authored code following the official gallery, and every D3 result below is labelled as such.\n150:| | Chart.js | Recharts | D3 (authored) | ECharts | Highcharts | ApexCharts | Plotly.js | Nivo | MUI X | uPlot |\n166:**R2. Labels that do not fit are the most common visible failure.** Only Highcharts wrapped \"Customer Success & Onboarding\". Recharts and MUI X removed that label and left a bar with no name; ECharts kept two labels of seven. Nivo, uPlot and the authored D3 chart overlapped text. ApexCharts and Plotly rotated the labels and they were cut at the container edge. Chart.js rotated all labels, and they stayed complete and readable.\n185:| D3 (authored) | UTC scale chosen in the code | | | – |\n191:| Chart.js, ECharts, Highcharts, ApexCharts, Plotly.js; Recharts (prints the string on a category axis); D3 (authored) | Nivo (its default tooltip prints a `Date` in local time); MUI X and uPlot (they accept only `Date` objects or numbers, so the string has to be converted first) |\n212:| D3 (authored, `.defined()` omitted on purpose) | line stops | line stops | plotted as 40 | line stops |\n243:**R10. Following the page's color scheme is rare, and following the device is not the same thing.** On a page that follows `prefers-color-scheme: dark`, two charts stayed legible without configuration: Highcharts 13, which switches palette and text, and the authored D3 chart, whose axis text uses the inherited color. On a page that declares itself light-only, viewed on a device set to dark, Highcharts drew a black chart on the white page: it follows the device preference, not the page. The other nine stayed light there. **Inferred**: a chart should take its scheme from the page it is in.\n254:| | Chart.js | Recharts | D3 (authored) | ECharts | Highcharts + module | ApexCharts | Plotly.js | Nivo | MUI X | uPlot |\n270:**R11. Half of the ten give a keyboard or assistive-technology user nothing to work with by default.** Chart.js and ECharts put one unlabeled canvas on the page. uPlot adds a title and a legend table that reads `--` until a mouse moves. Nivo is an unnamed image; the authored D3 chart is an image whose only content is its tick text. Chart.js' documentation leaves making the canvas accessible to the author (**Docs**, page dated 2021).\n316:| D3 | Everything: title, legend, readout and axes are authored, with no keyboard access. |\n353:| D3 (authored: one SVG path, x axis redrawn) | 27 ms | 87 ms | 521 ms | 1.1 ms | 0 |\n373:- The first version's D3 rows were invalid: the authored chart pinned its y axis at zero while the test data went negative, so most of the line was outside the plot. The rows above are from the corrected chart. Its y axis still does not follow the stream.\n382:**D10. One DOM node per point is slow; SVG as such is not.** Recharts and Nivo, which create an element per point by default, took 5 to 9 seconds at 100,000 points. The authored D3 chart and Plotly's default trace, which draw one SVG path per series, took about 0.1 s. Nivo's canvas component was not tested.\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
